var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#Hamiltonians","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"Number-conserving quadratic Hamiltonians, their builders, propagators, and the underlying numerical kernels. For BdG (pairing) Hamiltonians and their ground / thermal states, see States & Spectra.","title":"Hamiltonians"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#GaussianFermions.QuadraticHamiltonian","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"QuadraticHamiltonian{T} <: AbstractQuadraticHamiltonian\n\nA number-conserving quadratic Hamiltonian H = ∑ᵢⱼ hᵢⱼ c⁺ᵢ cⱼ, stored by its L×L Hermitian single-particle matrix h. Caches the last propagator exp(-i h dt) so repeated fixed-dt stepping does not re-diagonalize.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.QuadraticHamiltonian"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#GaussianFermions.QuadraticHamiltonian-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"QuadraticHamiltonian(h::AbstractMatrix)\n\nWrap a single-particle matrix h (Hermitized, element type ComplexF64).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.QuadraticHamiltonian"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#GaussianFermions.chemical_potential-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"chemical_potential(μ::AbstractVector)\n\nDiagonal on-site term H = ∑ᵢ μᵢ nᵢ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.chemical_potential"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#GaussianFermions.hopping-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Tuple}, Integer}","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"hopping(edges, L)\n\nGeneric hopping H = ∑ tᵢⱼ c⁺ᵢ cⱼ + h.c. from a list of (i, j, t) tuples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.hopping"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#GaussianFermions.hopping-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"hopping(L; J=1.0, pbc=false)\n\nNearest-neighbour hopping chain H = ∑ᵢ J c⁺ᵢ cᵢ₊₁ + h.c. on L sites.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.hopping"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#GaussianFermions.propagator-Tuple{QuadraticHamiltonian, Real}","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"propagator(H::QuadraticHamiltonian, dt) -> Matrix\n\nThe single-particle propagator U = exp(-i h dt) (memoized on H for the last dt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.propagator"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#GaussianFermions.avoid_vector!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"Compute null vectors:     |A'ᵢ⟩ = |Aᵢ⟩ - ⟨v|Aᵢ⟩|v⟩\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.avoid_vector!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#GaussianFermions.delete_vector-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"Given a vector space spanned by column of A, and a vector v, find the nullspace  of v inside A. The inpute vA is the pre-computed inner product ⟨v|Aᵢ⟩.\n\nThe algorithm first choose the pivot vector |Aₚ⟩ with largest overlap ‖⟨v|Aₚ⟩‖ with vector v, then calculate the vectors      |Ãᵢ⟩ := |Aᵢ⟩ - (⟨v|Aᵢ⟩/⟨v|Aₚ⟩) * |Aₚ⟩,  i ≠ p. The set of vectors {|Ãᵢ⟩} span the null space of v, though not orthogonal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.delete_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#GaussianFermions.evo_operator-Tuple{LinearAlgebra.Hermitian, Real}","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"evo_operator(H::Hermitian, dt::Real)\n\nExponential for Hermitian matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.evo_operator"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/hamiltonians/#GaussianFermions.rand_unitary-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"rand_unitary(N::Integer)\n\nGenerate random unitary matrix. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.rand_unitary"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"This page walks through the core workflow: prepare a state, evolve it, and measure observables. Every example is self-contained.","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"pkg> add https://github.com/jayren3996/GaussianFermions.jl\n\nusing GaussianFermions","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#1.-Prepare-a-state","page":"Getting Started","text":"A number-conserving free-fermion state is a SlaterState. Here a half-filled chain in a Z2 (alternating-occupation) product configuration:\n\nL = 8\ns = SlaterState(L=L, N=4, config=\"Z2\")\ndensity(s)            # site occupations\n\nFor correlation-matrix dynamics, wrap it in a CorrelationState:\n\nstate = CorrelationState(s)","title":"1. Prepare a state"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#2.-Build-a-Hamiltonian","page":"Getting Started","text":"Free-fermion (single-particle) Hamiltonians are QuadraticHamiltonians. Convenience builders construct common terms:\n\nH = hopping(L; pbc=true)          # nearest-neighbour hopping, periodic","title":"2. Build a Hamiltonian"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#3.-Evolve","page":"Getting Started","text":"evolve! advances a state in place. For unitary dynamics with a QuadraticHamiltonian:\n\nevolve!(state, H, 0.5)            # evolve for time t = 0.5\ndensity(state)\n\nFor repeated steps, precompute a propagator:\n\nU = propagator(H, 0.05)\nfor _ in 1:20\n    evolve!(state, U)\nend","title":"3. Evolve"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#4.-Measure-observables","page":"Getting Started","text":"density(state)                    # ⟨nᵢ⟩ per site\nparticle_number(state)            # total ⟨N⟩\nentanglement_entropy(state, 1:4)  # half-chain entanglement entropy","title":"4. Measure observables"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#5.-Add-dissipation","page":"Getting Started","text":"Open-system (Lindblad) dynamics for number-conserving channels use CorrelationLindblad:\n\nloss1 = zeros(ComplexF64, L); loss1[1] = sqrt(0.2)   # √γ · v for rate γ on mode v\nlind  = CorrelationLindblad(H; loss_ops=[loss1])\n\nstate = CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=L, N=4, config=\"Z2\"))\nevolve!(state, lind, 1.0)\ndensity(state)","title":"5. Add dissipation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Next-steps","page":"Getting Started","text":"States — SlaterState, CorrelationState, MajoranaState, and conversions between them.\nCorrelation-Matrix Lindblad and Majorana / BdG Foundation — open-system dynamics.\nQuantum Trajectories — monitored / measurement dynamics.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Observables","page":"Observables","text":"All observables of a Gaussian state are functions of its correlation / covariance matrix and are computed efficiently (polynomial in system size). They work uniformly across SlaterState, CorrelationState, and MajoranaState.","title":"Observables"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Densities-and-particle-number","page":"Observables","text":"state = CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=8, N=4, config=\"Z2\"))\n\ndensity(state)              # vector of site occupations ⟨nᵢ⟩\ndensity(state, i)           # occupation of a single site\ndensity_profile(state)      # site-resolved density profile\nparticle_number(state)      # total ⟨N⟩\nnumber_variance(state)      # Var(N)\nparity(state)               # fermion parity\npurity(state)               # Tr(ρ²)","title":"Densities and particle number"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Correlations","page":"Observables","text":"correlation_profile(state)  # spatial correlation profile\n\nFor Majorana states, both normal and anomalous correlations are available — see Majorana / BdG Foundation.","title":"Correlations"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Entanglement","page":"Observables","text":"Entanglement of a subregion A (given as an index range or vector) is obtained from the restricted correlation matrix:\n\nentanglement_entropy(state, 1:4)             # von Neumann entropy of region A\nrenyi_entropy(state, 1:4; α=2)               # Rényi-α entropy\nentanglement_spectrum(state, 1:4)            # single-particle entanglement spectrum\nentanglement_hamiltonian_spectrum(state, 1:4)\n\nMultipartite information measures:\n\nmutual_information(state, A, B)              # I(A:B)\ntripartite_information(state, A, B, C)       # I₃(A:B:C)\n\nThese are the standard diagnostics for monitored / measurement-induced transitions; a trajectory-averaged entanglement entropy is the order parameter in the Quantum Trajectories examples.\n\nSee the Observables API reference for full signatures.","title":"Entanglement"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/dynamics/#Dynamics","page":"Dynamics","text":"Time evolution, open-system (Lindblad) solvers, dissipation / measurement channels, and the low-level quantum-trajectory primitives — covering both the U(1) and Majorana / BdG layers.\n\nThis page collects the deterministic Lindblad generators (CorrelationLindblad, MajoranaLindblad), the dissipation / measurement channels, and the quantum-trajectory primitives (projective measurement, quantum-jump / MCWF, and continuous / QSD weak measurement).","title":"Dynamics"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.CorrelationLindblad","page":"Dynamics","text":"CorrelationLindblad(h; loss_ops=[], gain_ops=[], dephasing_ops=[])\nCorrelationLindblad(H::QuadraticHamiltonian; kwargs...)\n\nDeterministic correlation-matrix Lindblad generator for number-conserving (U(1)) open systems. The coherent part is the single-particle matrix h (or a QuadraticHamiltonian); dissipation is supplied as Lindblad amplitude vectors.\n\nFor a jump operator acting on a normalized mode v at rate γ, pass sqrt(γ) .* v:\n\nloss_ops — particle-loss amplitudes (∝ cᵥ),\ngain_ops — particle-gain amplitudes (∝ c⁺ᵥ),\ndephasing_ops — (mode, rate) pairs for occupation dephasing.\n\nEvolve a CorrelationState with evolve!(state, lind, dt), or solve directly for the unique attractor with steadystate. The deterministic solver is dense and intended for finite systems. See also MajoranaLindblad for the BdG / pairing generalisation, and lindblad_rhs for the bare right-hand side.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.CorrelationLindblad"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.lindblad_rhs-Tuple{CorrelationLindblad, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Dynamics","text":"lindblad_rhs(L::CorrelationLindblad, C) -> dC\n\nRight-hand side dC/dt of the correlation-matrix Lindblad equation for the generator L evaluated at correlation matrix C (or a CorrelationState). Useful for plugging the dynamics into a custom integrator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.lindblad_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.steadystate-Tuple{CorrelationLindblad}","page":"Dynamics","text":"steadystate(L::CorrelationLindblad; check_unique=true, atol=1e-10, rtol=1e-8) -> CorrelationState\n\nSolve directly for the steady state of the CorrelationLindblad generator L by finding the fixed point of the affine correlation-matrix dynamics. Valid for finite systems with a unique attractive steady state; with check_unique=true an error is raised if the dense generator is numerically singular (non-unique steady state). The result is validated against the master-equation residual and the physical occupation range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.steadystate"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.MajoranaLindblad-Tuple{BdGHamiltonian}","page":"Dynamics","text":"MajoranaLindblad(H; loss_ops=[], gain_ops=[], dephasing_ops=[], majorana_ops=[])\n\nDeterministic BdG covariance-matrix Lindblad generator. H is a BdGHamiltonian or a number-conserving QuadraticHamiltonian. The dissipators are:\n\nloss_ops  : Dirac amplitude vectors w (length L) for L = √γ already folded in, i.e. the jump operator Σᵢ wᵢ cᵢ (particle loss).\ngain_ops  : amplitude vectors for Σᵢ wᵢ cᵢ⁺ (particle gain).\ndephasing_ops : (mode_or_projector, γ) tuples (occupation monitoring), as in CorrelationLindblad.\nmajorana_ops : raw 2L-component complex Majorana jump vectors l for a general linear jump Σ_a l_a ω_a — use these for pairing/number-non-conserving baths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.MajoranaLindblad"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.MajoranaLindblad-Tuple{Union{BdGHamiltonian, QuadraticHamiltonian}, Any}","page":"Dynamics","text":"MajoranaLindblad(H, channels)\n\nBuild the generator from a list of AbstractChannels (Loss, Gain, OccupationMonitor, HoleMonitor), mirroring CorrelationLindblad(H, channels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.MajoranaLindblad"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.evolve!-Tuple{MajoranaState, MajoranaLindblad, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"evolve!(s::MajoranaState, Liouv::MajoranaLindblad, dt) -> s\n\nEvolve the covariance for time dt under the deterministic generator (dense affine matrix exponential). Mutates and returns s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.evolve!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.majorana_lindblad_rhs-Tuple{MajoranaLindblad, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Dynamics","text":"majorana_lindblad_rhs(Liouv, Γ) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nThe covariance time-derivative ∂ₜΓ (real antisymmetric).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.majorana_lindblad_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.steadystate-Tuple{MajoranaLindblad}","page":"Dynamics","text":"steadystate(Liouv::MajoranaLindblad; atol=1e-10, rtol=1e-8) -> MajoranaState\n\nSolve ∂ₜΓ = 0 for a unique steady covariance via the dense affine generator. Errors if the generator is numerically singular (non-unique steady state) or the solution is unphysical (eigenvalues of iΓ outside [-1, 1]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.steadystate"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.Gain","page":"Dynamics","text":"Gain(mode; γ=1) — L = √γ d⁺ (particle gain). Number-changing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.Gain"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.HoleMonitor","page":"Dynamics","text":"HoleMonitor(mode; γ=1) — L = √γ dd⁺ (hole-occupation measurement).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.HoleMonitor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.Loss","page":"Dynamics","text":"Loss(mode; γ=1) — L = √γ d (particle loss). Number-changing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.Loss"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.OccupationMonitor","page":"Dynamics","text":"OccupationMonitor(mode; γ=1) — L = √γ d⁺d (occupation measurement / dephasing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.OccupationMonitor"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.apply_noclick!-Tuple{GaussianFermions.AbstractChannel, SlaterState, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"apply_noclick!(ch, s, dt; normalize=true, threads=true)\n\nApply the no-jump back-action of ch for a step dt to a pure state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.apply_noclick!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.dephasing-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Dynamics","text":"dephasing(i, L; γ=1) — occupation monitor of site `i`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.dephasing"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.gain-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Dynamics","text":"gain(i, L; γ=1) — particle gain at site `i`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.gain"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.hole_exp-Tuple{QuasiMode, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"hole_exp(qm, γdt) — M₀ for L†L = dd† :  identity on d, √(1-γdt) elsewhere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.hole_exp"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.loss-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Dynamics","text":"loss(i, L; γ=1) — particle loss at site `i`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.loss"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.particle_exp-Tuple{QuasiMode, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"particle_exp(qm, γdt) — M₀ for L†L = d†d :  √(1-γdt) on d, identity elsewhere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.particle_exp"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.Feedback","page":"Dynamics","text":"Feedback(channel, on_click, on_noclick)\n\nA channel whose click / no-click outcome triggers a feedback unitary (on_click / on_noclick) on the channel's support.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.Feedback"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.Gate","page":"Dynamics","text":"Gate(M, I) — a local single-particle gate `M` acting on sites `I`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.Gate"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.NonHermitianGenerator","page":"Dynamics","text":"NonHermitianGenerator(Heff) — wraps the effective single-particle Hamiltonian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.NonHermitianGenerator"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.apply!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, SlaterState, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Dynamics","text":"apply!(M, s::SlaterState, inds; normalize=false, threads=false)\n\nApply a local single-particle matrix M on sites inds (in place, turbo kernels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.apply!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.apply!-Tuple{Feedback, SlaterState, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"apply!(fb::Feedback, s::SlaterState, dt; rng) -> Bool (whether a click occurred).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.apply!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.effective_hamiltonian-Tuple{QuadraticHamiltonian, Any}","page":"Dynamics","text":"effective_hamiltonian(H::QuadraticHamiltonian, channels) -> NonHermitianGenerator\n\nH_eff = h - (i/2) Σ L†L (single-particle), the generator of the no-click branch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.effective_hamiltonian"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.evolve!-Tuple{SlaterState, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Dynamics","text":"evolve!(s, U)            apply a single-particle unitary `U`\nevolve!(s, H, dt)        evolve for time `dt` under a `QuadraticHamiltonian`\nevolve(s, ...)           non-mutating copy\n\nPure (SlaterState): B ← U B. Mixed (CorrelationState): C ← U C U'.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.evolve!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.evolve_noclick!-Tuple{SlaterState, NonHermitianGenerator, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"evolve_noclick!(s::SlaterState, G, dt; renormalize=true) -> w\n\nDeterministic no-click evolution B ← exp(-i H_eff dt) B, returning the no-click weight w = ‖unnormalized‖² of the step (accumulate log(w) along a trajectory).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.evolve_noclick!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.measure!-Tuple{QuasiMode, SlaterState}","page":"Dynamics","text":"measure!(qm::QuasiMode, s::SlaterState; rng) -> Bool\n\nProjective occupation measurement of mode qm with Born statistics; returns the outcome (true = occupied).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.measure!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.noclick_propagator-Tuple{NonHermitianGenerator, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"noclick_propagator(G, dt) = exp(-i H_eff dt)  (non-unitary).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.noclick_propagator"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.weak_measure!-Tuple{SlaterState, Integer, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"weak_measure!(s::SlaterState, i::Integer, α::Real) -> s\n\nApply the weak-measurement Gaussian filter exp(α nᵢ) to site i and renormalize (B[i,:] ← e^α B[i,:]). For continuous (quantum-state-diffusion) occupation monitoring at rate γ, step it in a loop with α = δW + (2⟨nᵢ⟩ − 1) γ dt, δW ~ 𝒩(0, γ dt); averaging over the noise reproduces the dephasing Lindblad D[√γ nᵢ]. Matches the MajoranaState weak_measure! to machine precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.weak_measure!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.weak_measure!-Tuple{SlaterState, QuasiMode, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"weak_measure!(s::SlaterState, qm::QuasiMode, α::Real) -> s\n\nWeak-measurement filter exp(α nₘ) for the occupation nₘ = d†d of quasi-mode qm (d = Σₐ qm.Vₐ c_{qm.Iₐ}); B[qm.I,:] ← [(e^α-1) V V' + I] B[qm.I,:], renormalized. Read ⟨nₘ⟩ with density(s, qm) to form α in a QSD loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.weak_measure!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#LinearAlgebra.normalize!-Tuple{SlaterState}","page":"Dynamics","text":"normalize!(s::SlaterState) — re-orthonormalize the orbitals via QR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.normalize!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.apply_click!-Tuple{MajoranaState, AbstractVector}","page":"Dynamics","text":"apply_click!(s::MajoranaState, ℓ::AbstractVector) -> s\n\nApply a definite quantum-jump (click) of the linear jump ℓ: render the conditional Gaussian state L|ψ⟩/‖L|ψ⟩‖ on the covariance. Errors if the jump rate is zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.apply_click!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.apply_noclick!-Tuple{MajoranaState, Any, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"apply_noclick!(s::MajoranaState, jumps, dt) -> s\n\nApply the MCWF no-click back-action of the linear jumps (an iterable of Majorana jump vectors ℓ) over a step dt, by RK4-integrating the effective non-Hermitian drift. Pair with jump_rate/apply_click! in a trajectory loop: draw at most one click over dt (probability Σ jump_rate*dt), otherwise call this. The click/no-click average reproduces MajoranaLindblad built from the same jumps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.apply_noclick!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.gain_jump-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Dynamics","text":"gain_jump(w) -> ℓ — Majorana jump vector of particle gain `L = Σᵢ wᵢ cᵢ⁺`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.gain_jump"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.jump_rate-Tuple{MajoranaState, AbstractVector}","page":"Dynamics","text":"jump_rate(s::MajoranaState, ℓ::AbstractVector) -> Float64\n\nInstantaneous click rate ⟨L†L⟩ = ℓ†(I − iΓ)ℓ of the linear jump with Majorana vector ℓ (build ℓ with loss_jump/gain_jump/majorana_jump). The click probability over a step dt is jump_rate(s, ℓ) * dt. (Contrast the SlaterState channel form jump_rate(ch, s, dt), which returns a probability and a work vector.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.jump_rate"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.loss_jump-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Dynamics","text":"loss_jump(w) -> ℓ — Majorana jump vector of particle loss `L = Σᵢ wᵢ cᵢ`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.loss_jump"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.majorana_jump-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Dynamics","text":"majorana_jump(annihilate, create) -> Vector{ComplexF64}\n\nMajorana jump vector ℓ (length 2L) of the linear Dirac jump L = Σᵢ annihilateᵢ cᵢ + createᵢ cᵢ⁺, in the basis cᵢ = (xᵢ − i pᵢ)/2, ω = [x₁…x_L, p₁…p_L]. Pass ℓ to jump_rate / apply_click!. For a rate-γ jump fold √γ into the amplitudes. Use loss_jump / gain_jump for the pure loss/gain cases, this for general pairing baths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.majorana_jump"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.measure!-Tuple{MajoranaState, Integer}","page":"Dynamics","text":"measure!(s::MajoranaState, i::Integer; rng=Random.default_rng()) -> Bool\n\nProjectively measure the occupation of mode i with Born statistics (P(occupied) = ⟨nᵢ⟩), collapse the state, and return the outcome (true = occupied). The covariance is updated by the fermionic-Gaussian measurement rule: the measured pair becomes a definite-occupation block and the remaining modes are conditioned by a Schur complement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.measure!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/dynamics/#GaussianFermions.weak_measure!-Tuple{MajoranaState, Integer, Real}","page":"Dynamics","text":"weak_measure!(s::MajoranaState, i::Integer, α::Real) -> s\n\nApply the exact weak-measurement Gaussian filter exp(α nᵢ) to the covariance and renormalize (α → ±∞ recovers projective occupation/vacancy). For continuous (quantum-state-diffusion) occupation monitoring at rate γ, step it in a loop with α = δW + (2⟨nᵢ⟩ − 1) γ dt, δW ~ 𝒩(0, γ dt); averaging over the noise reproduces the dephasing Lindblad D[√γ nᵢ]. (Only site occupation is supported here; unlike the SlaterState filter there is no general quasi-mode variant.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.weak_measure!"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/hamiltonians/#Hamiltonians-and-Time-Evolution","page":"Hamiltonians & Time Evolution","text":"","title":"Hamiltonians & Time Evolution"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/hamiltonians/#Quadratic-(number-conserving)-Hamiltonians","page":"Hamiltonians & Time Evolution","text":"A free-fermion Hamiltonian H = sum_ij h_ij c_i^dagger c_j is a QuadraticHamiltonian, wrapping the single-particle matrix h. Convenience builders construct common terms:\n\nH = hopping(8; pbc=true)                 # nearest-neighbour hopping\nμ = chemical_potential(8; μ=0.5)         # on-site chemical potential\n\nThese return QuadraticHamiltonians whose single-particle matrices can be combined.","title":"Quadratic (number-conserving) Hamiltonians"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/hamiltonians/#Unitary-evolution","page":"Hamiltonians & Time Evolution","text":"evolve! advances a state in place under a Hamiltonian for a given time:\n\nstate = CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=8, N=4, config=\"Z2\"))\nevolve!(state, H, 0.5)\n\nFor repeated time steps, precompute a propagator (the single-particle evolution operator e^-iht) and reuse it:\n\nU = propagator(H, 0.05)\nfor _ in 1:40\n    evolve!(state, U)\nend\n\nevolve (without the bang) returns a new evolved state instead of mutating in place.","title":"Unitary evolution"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/hamiltonians/#BdG-(pairing)-Hamiltonians","page":"Hamiltonians & Time Evolution","text":"A general quadratic Hamiltonian with pairing, H = sum_ij A_ij c_i^dagger c_j + tfrac12 (B_ij c_i^dagger c_j^dagger + texthc), is a BdGHamiltonian built from the Hermitian hopping matrix A and the antisymmetric pairing matrix B:\n\nA = zeros(ComplexF64, 4, 4)\nB = zeros(ComplexF64, 4, 4); B[1, 2] = 0.3; B[2, 1] = -0.3\nH = BdGHamiltonian(A, B)\n\nA number-conserving QuadraticHamiltonian lifts into a BdGHamiltonian directly:\n\nH = BdGHamiltonian(QuadraticHamiltonian(Matrix(hopping(8; pbc=true).h)))\n\nBdGHamiltonian evolves a MajoranaState's covariance under dense unitary dynamics, and supports the same propagator / evolve! interface:\n\nstate = MajoranaState([1, 0, 1, 0])\nevolve!(state, H, 0.5)","title":"BdG (pairing) Hamiltonians"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/hamiltonians/#Ground-and-thermal-states","page":"Hamiltonians & Time Evolution","text":"Ground and thermal states of a BdGHamiltonian come from Bogoliubov / Nambu diagonalization:\n\ngs = groundstate(H)                      # BCS ground state as a MajoranaState\nε  = quasiparticle_energies(H)           # Bogoliubov spectrum (≥ 0)\nρβ = thermalstate(H; β=2.0)              # Gibbs state; β → ∞ recovers groundstate(H)\n\nSee the Hamiltonians API reference and the States & Spectra API reference.","title":"Ground and thermal states"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/overview/#API-Reference","page":"Overview","text":"This section documents the public API of GaussianFermions.jl, grouped by topic:\n\nStates & Spectra — SlaterState, CorrelationState, MajoranaState, BdGHamiltonian, quasi-modes, and ground / thermal states.\nHamiltonians — QuadraticHamiltonian, builders, and propagators.\nObservables — densities, particle-number statistics, and entanglement diagnostics.\nDynamics — CorrelationLindblad, MajoranaLindblad, dissipation / measurement channels, and quantum-trajectory primitives.","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/overview/#Index","page":"Overview","text":"Modules = [GaussianFermions]","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/correlation-lindblad/#Correlation-Matrix-Lindblad","page":"Correlation-Matrix Lindblad","text":"For number-conserving open systems, CorrelationLindblad evolves the single-particle correlation matrix C under a quadratic Lindblad master equation with linear (loss / gain) and quadratic (dephasing) jump operators. The dynamics stay closed at the level of C, so the cost is polynomial in system size.","title":"Correlation-Matrix Lindblad"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/correlation-lindblad/#Building-a-Lindbladian","page":"Correlation-Matrix Lindblad","text":"using GaussianFermions\n\nL = 16\nH = hopping(L; pbc=true)\n\nloss1 = zeros(ComplexF64, L); loss1[1] = sqrt(0.2)   # particle loss at site 1\nlind  = CorrelationLindblad(H; loss_ops=[loss1])\n\nThe loss_ops and gain_ops keywords take Lindblad amplitude vectors: for rate gamma acting on a normalized mode v, pass sqrtgammav. The Hamiltonian H supplies the coherent part.","title":"Building a Lindbladian"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/correlation-lindblad/#Time-evolution","page":"Correlation-Matrix Lindblad","text":"state = CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=L, N=8, config=\"Z2\"))\nevolve!(state, lind, 1.0)\ndensity(state)\n\nThe deterministic solver is dense and intended for finite systems. The right-hand side of the master equation is exposed as lindblad_rhs if you want to plug it into a custom integrator.","title":"Time evolution"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/correlation-lindblad/#Steady-states","page":"Correlation-Matrix Lindblad","text":"For a finite system with a unique attractive steady state, solve for it directly with steadystate instead of integrating to long times:\n\nsteady = steadystate(CorrelationLindblad(zeros(ComplexF64, 1, 1);\n                                         loss_ops=[ComplexF64[sqrt(0.6)]],\n                                         gain_ops=[ComplexF64[sqrt(0.2)]]))\ndensity(steady)   # ≈ [0.25]","title":"Steady states"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/correlation-lindblad/#Relation-to-other-solvers","page":"Correlation-Matrix Lindblad","text":"CorrelationLindblad is the number-conserving specialisation of MajoranaLindblad, which additionally handles pairing (number-non-conserving) baths. On number-conserving channels the two agree to machine precision (see example/Dephase.jl). The same dynamics can also be unravelled into quantum trajectories.\n\nSee the Dynamics API reference.","title":"Relation to other solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/states/#States","page":"States","text":"GaussianFermions.jl represents fermionic Gaussian states in two complementary ways.","title":"States"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/states/#Number-conserving-(U(1))-states","page":"States","text":"These describe states with a fixed (or fluctuating but U(1)-symmetric) particle number, fully characterised by the single-particle correlation matrix C_ij = langle c_i^dagger c_jrangle.","title":"Number-conserving (U(1)) states"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/states/#SlaterState","page":"States","text":"A pure Slater determinant, stored by its occupied single-particle orbitals.\n\ns = SlaterState(L=8, N=4, config=\"Z2\")   # half-filled, alternating occupation\nnmodes(s)                                # 8\nispure(s)                                # true\n\nThe config keyword selects the initial product configuration (e.g. \"Z2\" for alternating occupation). You can also build a SlaterState directly from a set of orbitals.","title":"SlaterState"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/states/#CorrelationState","page":"States","text":"A general (possibly mixed) number-conserving Gaussian state, stored by its correlation matrix. Construct it from a SlaterState, or from a correlation matrix directly:\n\nstate = CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=8, N=4, config=\"Z2\"))\ncorrelation_matrix(state)                # the L×L matrix Cᵢⱼ\ncorrelation(state, i, j)                 # a single entry\n\nMixed reference states are available as helpers:\n\nthermalstate(H; β=2.0)                   # Gibbs state of a QuadraticHamiltonian\nmaximally_mixed(L)                       # infinite-temperature state","title":"CorrelationState"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/states/#General-quadratic-(BdG-/-Majorana)-states","page":"States","text":"When pairing terms are present, particle number is no longer conserved and the state is described by the real antisymmetric Majorana covariance matrix Gamma_ab = tfraci2langleomega_a omega_brangle.","title":"General quadratic (BdG / Majorana) states"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/states/#MajoranaState","page":"States","text":"state = MajoranaState([1, 0, 1, 0])      # product state from occupation pattern\ncovariance_matrix(state)                 # the 2L×2L covariance Γ\n\nFrom a MajoranaState you can recover both normal and anomalous correlations:\n\nnormal_correlation(state)                # Cᵢⱼ = ⟨c⁺ᵢcⱼ⟩\nanomalous_correlation(state)             # Fᵢⱼ = ⟨cᵢcⱼ⟩\nfermion_correlations(state)              # both, as a Nambu-structured object","title":"MajoranaState"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/states/#Converting-between-representations","page":"States","text":"A number-conserving CorrelationState embeds into the Majorana representation:\n\nnc   = CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=4, N=2, config=\"Z2\"))\nmaj  = MajoranaState(nc)                 # same physical state, covariance form\n\nThis conversion is the bridge used throughout the package: prepare a state with the convenient SlaterState / CorrelationState API, then lift it into MajoranaState when pairing dynamics are required.\n\nSee the States & Spectra API reference for full signatures.","title":"Converting between representations"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/majorana-bdg/#Majorana-/-BdG-Foundation","page":"Majorana / BdG Foundation","text":"The Majorana layer handles general quadratic dynamics, including pairing (number-non- conserving) terms that the correlation-matrix layer cannot represent. States live in MajoranaState (the real antisymmetric covariance matrix Gamma), and dynamics are generated by BdGHamiltonian and MajoranaLindblad.","title":"Majorana / BdG Foundation"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/majorana-bdg/#States-and-correlations","page":"Majorana / BdG Foundation","text":"using GaussianFermions\n\nstate = MajoranaState([1, 0, 1, 0])\nnormal_correlation(state)        # Cᵢⱼ = ⟨c⁺ᵢcⱼ⟩\nanomalous_correlation(state)     # Fᵢⱼ = ⟨cᵢcⱼ⟩\n\nCorrelations follow the standard convention C_ij = langle c_i^dagger c_jrangle, F_ij = langle c_i c_jrangle with covariance Gamma_ab = tfraci2langleomega_a omega_brangle (verified against exact diagonalization).","title":"States and correlations"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/majorana-bdg/#Unitary-BdG-dynamics","page":"Majorana / BdG Foundation","text":"BdGHamiltonian is built from a Hermitian hopping matrix A and an antisymmetric pairing matrix B, and evolves a MajoranaState's covariance:\n\nA = zeros(ComplexF64, 4, 4)\nB = zeros(ComplexF64, 4, 4); B[1, 2] = 0.3; B[2, 1] = -0.3\n\nH = BdGHamiltonian(A, B)\nevolve!(state, H, 0.5)\n\nGround and thermal states come from Bogoliubov / Nambu diagonalization:\n\ngs = groundstate(H)              # BCS ground state as a MajoranaState\nε  = quasiparticle_energies(H)   # Bogoliubov spectrum (≥ 0)\nρβ = thermalstate(H; β=2.0)      # Gibbs state; β → ∞ recovers groundstate(H)","title":"Unitary BdG dynamics"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/majorana-bdg/#Open-system-BdG-dynamics","page":"Majorana / BdG Foundation","text":"MajoranaLindblad is the covariance-matrix analogue of CorrelationLindblad. It supports particle loss / gain, occupation dephasing, and — unlike the number-conserving solver — general pairing baths via raw Majorana jump vectors:\n\nH = hopping(4; pbc=true)\nlind = MajoranaLindblad(H; loss_ops=[sqrt(0.3) * ComplexF64[1, 0, 0, 0]],\n                        dephasing_ops=[(ComplexF64[1, 1, 0, 0] / sqrt(2), 0.5)])\n\nstate = MajoranaState(CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=4, N=2, config=\"Z2\")))\nevolve!(state, lind, 1.0)\n\ndensity(state)\nnormal_correlation(state)\nanomalous_correlation(state)     # nonzero once a pairing bath acts\n\ndephasing_ops entries are (mode, rate) pairs. The master-equation right-hand side is exposed as majorana_lindblad_rhs.\n\nOn number-conserving channels MajoranaLindblad reproduces CorrelationLindblad to machine precision (see example/Dephase.jl).\n\nSee the States & Spectra API reference and the Dynamics API reference.","title":"Open-system BdG dynamics"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/trajectories/#Quantum-Trajectories","page":"Quantum Trajectories","text":"Monitored dynamics are built from low-level primitives — there is no built-in trajectory runner. You own the loop: evolve a step, decide measurements / jumps, and accumulate observables. This keeps the dynamics explicit and tunable per script.\n\nThe primitives work for both SlaterState and MajoranaState:\n\nProjective occupation measurement (Born statistics, collapse): measure!(s::MajoranaState, i) measures site i; measure!(qm::QuasiMode, s::SlaterState) measures a quasi-mode of a SlaterState.\nQuantum-jump (MCWF) building blocks: jump_rate(s, ℓ), apply_click!(s, ℓ), apply_noclick!(s, jumps, dt). For SlaterState the channel forms are jump_rate(ch, s, dt), apply_click!(ch, s, work), apply_noclick!(ch, s, dt).\nContinuous (QSD) weak measurement: weak_measure!(s, i, α) applies the exact weak-measurement filter e^alpha n_i.\n\nThe jump vectors are built with loss_jump, gain_jump, and majorana_jump.","title":"Quantum Trajectories"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/trajectories/#Projective-monitoring","page":"Quantum Trajectories","text":"Projective measurement of n_i at rate gamma averages to the dephasing Lindblad mathcalDsqrt2gamma n_i, so a trajectory average reproduces MajoranaLindblad. A hand-rolled trajectory average of half-chain entanglement:\n\nusing GaussianFermions, Random\n\nH = BdGHamiltonian(QuadraticHamiltonian(Matrix(hopping(8; pbc=true).h)))\nU = propagator(H, 0.05)\nrng = Xoshiro(1); ntraj = 200; ΣS = 0.0\nfor _ in 1:ntraj\n    s = MajoranaState(CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=8, N=4, config=\"Z2\")))\n    for _ in 1:40\n        evolve!(s, U)\n        for i in 1:8\n            rand(rng) < 0.5 * 0.05 && measure!(s, i; rng)   # monitor each site at rate 0.5\n        end\n    end\n    global ΣS += entanglement_entropy(s, 1:4)\nend\nΣS / ntraj   # trajectory-averaged half-chain entanglement","title":"Projective monitoring"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/trajectories/#Dissipative-(loss-/-gain-/-pairing)-jumps","page":"Quantum Trajectories","text":"A Monte-Carlo wave-function (MCWF) step either draws a click (the conditional Gaussian state) or applies the effective non-Hermitian no-click drift. Pairing jumps generate anomalous correlations:\n\nusing GaussianFermions, Random\n\nU = propagator(BdGHamiltonian(QuadraticHamiltonian(Matrix(hopping(4; pbc=true).h))), 0.01)\njumps = [loss_jump(sqrt(0.4) * ComplexF64[1, 0, 0, 0]),\n         gain_jump(sqrt(0.25) * ComplexF64[0, 0, 1, 0])]\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\ns = MajoranaState(CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=4, N=2, config=\"Z2\")))\nfor _ in 1:150\n    evolve!(s, U)\n    rates = [jump_rate(s, ℓ) for ℓ in jumps]\n    if rand(rng) < sum(rates) * 0.01\n        apply_click!(s, jumps[argmax(rand(rng) .< cumsum(rates) ./ sum(rates))])\n    else\n        apply_noclick!(s, jumps, 0.01)\n    end\nend\ndensity(s)   # averaged over trajectories, matches MajoranaLindblad(H; loss_ops=…, gain_ops=…)","title":"Dissipative (loss / gain / pairing) jumps"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/trajectories/#Continuous-(QSD)-monitoring","page":"Quantum Trajectories","text":"Continuous monitoring applies the exact weak filter e^alpha_i n_i with alpha_i = delta W_i + (2langle n_irangle - 1)gamma_i dt:\n\nusing GaussianFermions, Random\n\nU = propagator(BdGHamiltonian(QuadraticHamiltonian(Matrix(hopping(4; pbc=true).h))), 0.01)\nrng = Xoshiro(1); γ = 0.7\ns = MajoranaState(CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=4, N=2, config=\"Z2\")))\nfor _ in 1:100\n    evolve!(s, U)\n    for i in 1:4\n        α = randn(rng) * sqrt(γ * 0.01) + (2 * density(s, i) - 1) * γ * 0.01\n        weak_measure!(s, i, α)\n    end\nend\ndensity(s)\n\nThe MajoranaState weak_measure! matches the number-conserving SlaterState one to machine precision, and averaging over the noise reproduces the dephasing Lindblad mathcalDsqrtgamma n_i (verified against exact diagonalization, including paired states).","title":"Continuous (QSD) monitoring"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/trajectories/#Complete-examples","page":"Quantum Trajectories","text":"See example/FreeFermion.jl, example/MI.jl, and example/Dephase.jl for full trajectory + averaging loops.\n\nThe trajectory primitives are documented in the Dynamics API reference.","title":"Complete examples"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/states/#States-and-Spectra","page":"States & Spectra","text":"State types for both the number-conserving (U(1)) and general quadratic (BdG / Majorana) representations, together with the spectral constructions (ground / thermal states, quasiparticle energies) that produce them.","title":"States & Spectra"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.AbstractGaussianState","page":"States & Spectra","text":"NumberConservingGaussianState{T}\n\nAbstract supertype for number-conserving (U(1)-symmetric) fermionic Gaussian states on L single-particle modes. Such a state is fully characterised by the single-particle correlation matrix\n\nCᵢⱼ = ⟨c⁺ᵢ cⱼ⟩            (L×L, Hermitian, eigenvalues in [0,1]).\n\nPure states have C a projector (C² = C); mixed states are general.\n\nInterface every subtype implements:     correlationmatrix(s)     -> L×L Hermitian C     nmodes(s)                 -> L Optional fast paths a subtype may specialise:     correlationmatrix(s, A)  -> restricted block C[A,A]     correlation(s, i, j)      -> scalar Cᵢⱼ\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.AbstractGaussianState"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.CorrelationState","page":"States & Spectra","text":"CorrelationState{T} <: NumberConservingGaussianState{T}\n\nGeneral (possibly mixed) number-conserving Gaussian state, stored by its full L×L correlation matrix Cᵢⱼ = ⟨c⁺ᵢ cⱼ⟩ (Hermitian, spectrum ⊆ [0,1]). Use for finite-temperature/Gibbs states and (later) Lindblad steady states. Pure states are the special case C² = C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.CorrelationState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.CorrelationState-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"CorrelationState(C::AbstractMatrix)\n\nWrap a correlation matrix (stored as Hermitian, element type ComplexF64).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.CorrelationState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.CorrelationState-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Integer}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"CorrelationState(pos::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, L::Integer)\n\nFock state (as a correlation state) with sites pos occupied on L modes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.CorrelationState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.CorrelationState-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"CorrelationState(occ::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nDiagonal correlation matrix from site occupations occ ∈ [0,1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.CorrelationState"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.SlaterState","page":"States & Spectra","text":"SlaterState{T} <: NumberConservingGaussianState{T}\n\nPure number-conserving Gaussian state (single Slater determinant), stored by its L×N orthonormal orbital matrix B:\n\n|ψ⟩ = ∏ₖ b⁺ₖ |0⟩,   b⁺ₖ = ∑ᵢ c⁺ᵢ Bᵢₖ.\n\nColumns of B are orthonormal (B'B = I); the correlation matrix is the rank-N projector C = conj(B) * transpose(B). This is the efficient representation for trajectory simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.SlaterState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.SlaterState-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"SlaterState(B::AbstractMatrix; orthonormalize=false)\n\nWrap an orbital matrix directly. With orthonormalize=true the columns are re-orthonormalized via QR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.SlaterState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.SlaterState-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Integer}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"SlaterState(pos, L; dtype=ComplexF64)\n\nFock state with the sites in pos occupied on L modes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.SlaterState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.SlaterState-Tuple{AbstractVector{Bool}}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"SlaterState(occ::AbstractVector{Bool}; dtype=ComplexF64)\n\nFock state from a Boolean occupation vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.SlaterState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.SlaterState-Tuple{CorrelationState}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"SlaterState(s::CorrelationState; tol=1e-8)\n\nRecover the orbital form of a pure correlation state (eigenvectors with occupation ≈ 1 become the columns of B). Errors if s is not pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.SlaterState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.SlaterState-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"SlaterState(L::Integer, N::Integer; rng=Random.default_rng())\n\nRandom Slater determinant of N particles on L modes (first N columns of a Haar-random unitary).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.SlaterState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.SlaterState-Tuple{LinearAlgebra.Hermitian, Integer}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"SlaterState(h::Hermitian, N)\n\nGround state at filling N of the quadratic Hamiltonian H = ∑ hᵢⱼ c⁺ᵢ cⱼ: the lowest N single-particle eigen-orbitals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.SlaterState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.SlaterState-Tuple{}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"SlaterState(; L, N, config=\"Z2\")\n\nProduct-state presets on L modes with N particles. config ∈ \"left\", \"right\", \"center\", \"random\", or \"Zn\" (e.g. \"Z2\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.SlaterState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.correlation-Tuple{SlaterState, Integer, Integer}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"correlation(s, i, j) -> Number\n\nTwo-point function ⟨c⁺ᵢ cⱼ⟩.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.correlation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.correlation_matrix-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"correlation_matrix(s, A) -> Matrix\n\nRestricted block C[A,A] (the basis of entanglement diagnostics).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.correlation_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.correlation_matrix-Tuple{SlaterState}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"correlation_matrix(s) -> Matrix\n\nFull correlation matrix Cᵢⱼ = ⟨c⁺ᵢ cⱼ⟩ (Hermitian, eigenvalues ∈ [0,1]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.correlation_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.ispure-Tuple{SlaterState}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"ispure(s; tol=1e-8) -> Bool\n\nWhether the state is pure (C² ≈ C, i.e. all occupations ≈ 0 or 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.ispure"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.maximally_mixed-Tuple{Integer}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"maximally_mixed(L; filling=0.5)\n\nMaximally mixed state at the given uniform filling: C = filling · I.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.maximally_mixed"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.nmodes-Tuple{SlaterState}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"nmodes(s) -> Int\n\nNumber of single-particle modes L.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.nmodes"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.thermalstate-Tuple{LinearAlgebra.Hermitian}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"thermalstate(h::Hermitian; β, μ=0)\n\nGibbs state of H = ∑ hᵢⱼ c⁺ᵢ cⱼ at inverse temperature β and chemical potential μ: C = U f(β(ε-μ)) U' with Fermi–Dirac occupations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.thermalstate"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.QuasiMode","page":"States & Spectra","text":"QuasiMode(I, V, L)\n\nA single-particle (\"quasi\") mode\n\nd⁺ = ∑ⱼ Vⱼ c⁺_{Iⱼ}\n\non L sites, stored sparsely by the support indices I and amplitudes V. For a normalized mode ‖V‖ = 1, so that d⁺d is a projector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.QuasiMode"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.inner!-Tuple{AbstractVector, QuasiMode, AbstractMatrix}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"inner!(out, qm, B) ; inner(qm, B)\n\nOverlaps of the mode d with each occupied orbital (column of B): outₖ = ⟨d | Bₖ⟩ = ∑ⱼ conj(Vⱼ) B[Iⱼ, k].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.inner!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.vector-Tuple{QuasiMode}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"vector(qm::QuasiMode) -> SparseVector\n\nMaterialize the mode amplitudes as a length-L sparse vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.vector"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.MajoranaState","page":"States & Spectra","text":"MajoranaState{T} <: AbstractGaussianState{T}\n\nGeneral (possibly mixed, possibly paired) fermionic Gaussian state on L modes, stored by its real antisymmetric 2L×2L Majorana covariance matrix\n\nΓ_ab = (i/2)⟨[ωₐ, ω_b]⟩,   ω = [x₁…x_L, p₁…p_L],  xⱼ = cⱼ+c⁺ⱼ,  pⱼ = i(cⱼ-c⁺ⱼ).\n\nT<:AbstractFloat is the covariance element type (Float64 by default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.MajoranaState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.MajoranaState-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"MajoranaState(occ::AbstractVector{<:Integer})\n\nProduct (Fock) state from an occupation vector occ of 0/1 entries: Γ[i, i+L] = 1 - 2 occᵢ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.MajoranaState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.MajoranaState-Tuple{CorrelationState}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"MajoranaState(s::CorrelationState; check=true, atol=1e-10)\n\nCovariance form of a (possibly mixed) number-conserving state: builds Γ from the correlation matrix Cᵢⱼ = ⟨c⁺ᵢcⱼ⟩ with zero anomalous part Fᵢⱼ = ⟨cᵢcⱼ⟩ = 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.MajoranaState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.MajoranaState-Tuple{SlaterState}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"MajoranaState(s::SlaterState) — covariance form of a pure number-conserving state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.MajoranaState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.MajoranaState-Union{Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"States & Spectra","text":"MajoranaState(Γ::AbstractMatrix; check=true, atol=1e-10)\n\nWrap a Majorana covariance matrix Γ (real, antisymmetric, spectrum of iΓ in [-1,1]). A floating-point element type (Float32/Float64/BigFloat) is preserved; integer/other inputs are promoted to Float64. With check=false the physicality validation is skipped (use only for covariances you trust).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.MajoranaState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.ispure-Tuple{MajoranaState}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"ispure(s::MajoranaState; tol=1e-8) -> Bool\n\nWhether the covariance state is pure: all eigenvalues of iΓ are ≈ ±1 (equivalently Γ² ≈ -I).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.ispure"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.groundstate-Tuple{BdGHamiltonian}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"groundstate(H::BdGHamiltonian) -> MajoranaState\ngroundstate(H::QuadraticHamiltonian) -> MajoranaState\n\nGround state of the quadratic Hamiltonian as a Majorana covariance state, via Bogoliubov/Nambu diagonalization (occupy the positive-energy Nambu modes). For a number-conserving H this is the Fermi sea (all negative single-particle orbitals filled).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.groundstate"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.quasiparticle_energies-Tuple{BdGHamiltonian}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"quasiparticle_energies(H::BdGHamiltonian) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe non-negative Bogoliubov quasiparticle energies (the ≥ 0 half of the particle-hole-symmetric Nambu spectrum), sorted ascending.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.quasiparticle_energies"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/states/#GaussianFermions.thermalstate-Tuple{BdGHamiltonian}","page":"States & Spectra","text":"thermalstate(H::BdGHamiltonian; β) -> MajoranaState\n\nGibbs state ρ ∝ exp(-β Ĥ) of a BdG Hamiltonian as a Majorana covariance state. β → ∞ recovers groundstate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.thermalstate"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/observables/#Observables","page":"Observables","text":"Densities, particle-number statistics, correlations, and entanglement diagnostics. These work uniformly across SlaterState, CorrelationState, and MajoranaState.","title":"Observables"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions._occupation_spectrum-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState, Any}","page":"Observables","text":"_occupation_spectrum(s, A) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nSingle-particle entanglement spectrum of region A: eigenvalues of the restricted correlation block C_A, clamped to [0,1] (kills -1e-16 / 1+1e-16).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions._occupation_spectrum"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.correlation_profile-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState, Integer}","page":"Observables","text":"correlation_profile(s, i) -> Vector\n\nThe correlations ⟨c⁺ᵢ cⱼ⟩ from a reference site i to every site j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.correlation_profile"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.density-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState}","page":"Observables","text":"density(s)      -> Vector{Float64}\ndensity(s, i)   -> Float64\n\nSite occupations ⟨nᵢ⟩ = ⟨c⁺ᵢ cᵢ⟩.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.density"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.density_profile-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState}","page":"Observables","text":"density_profile(s) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nAlias for density (occupation as a function of site).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.density_profile"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.entanglement_entropy-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Observables","text":"entanglement_entropy(s, A) -> Float64\n\nVon Neumann entanglement entropy of region A, S(A) = -∑ₖ [νₖ log νₖ + (1-νₖ) log(1-νₖ)], where {νₖ} are the eigenvalues of C_A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.entanglement_entropy"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.entanglement_hamiltonian_spectrum-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Observables","text":"entanglement_hamiltonian_spectrum(s, A) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nEntanglement-Hamiltonian single-particle levels εₖ = log((1-νₖ)/νₖ) (±Inf for νₖ = 0 or 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.entanglement_hamiltonian_spectrum"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.entanglement_spectrum-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Observables","text":"entanglement_spectrum(s, A) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nSingle-particle entanglement spectrum: the occupations νₖ ∈ [0,1] of C_A (sorted ascending).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.entanglement_spectrum"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.mutual_information-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Observables","text":"mutual_information(s, A, B; α=1) -> Float64\n\nI(A:B) = S(A) + S(B) - S(A∪B) (Rényi version uses Sα).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.mutual_information"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.number_variance-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Observables","text":"number_variance(s, A) -> Float64\n\nCharge fluctuation Var(N_A) = ∑ₖ νₖ(1-νₖ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.number_variance"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.parity","page":"Observables","text":"parity(s, A=1:nmodes(s)) -> Float64\n\nFermion parity ⟨(-1)^{N_A}⟩ = det(I - 2 C_A).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.parity"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.particle_number-Tuple{SlaterState}","page":"Observables","text":"particle_number(s)      -> total ⟨N⟩\nparticle_number(s, A)   -> ⟨N_A⟩\n\nMean particle number. For a SlaterState the total is the exact integer N.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.particle_number"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.purity","page":"Observables","text":"purity(s, A=1:nmodes(s)) -> Float64\n\nTr ρ_A² = ∏ₖ (νₖ² + (1-νₖ)²). Equals 1 for a pure global state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.purity"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.renyi_entropy-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Observables","text":"renyi_entropy(s, A; α=2) -> Float64\n\nRényi-α entanglement entropy Sα(A) = (1/(1-α)) ∑ₖ log(νₖ^α + (1-νₖ)^α). α == 1 returns the von Neumann entropy; α == Inf the min-entropy -∑ₖ log max(νₖ, 1-νₖ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.renyi_entropy"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/observables/#GaussianFermions.tripartite_information-Tuple{NumberConservingGaussianState, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Observables","text":"tripartite_information(s, A, B, C; α=1) -> Float64\n\nI₃ = S(A)+S(B)+S(C) - S(AB) - S(AC) - S(BC) + S(ABC) — the order parameter for measurement-induced transitions. A, B, C must be disjoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussianFermions.tripartite_information"},{"category":"section","location":"#GaussianFermions.jl","page":"Home","text":"Simulation of fermionic Gaussian states under unitary, Lindblad, and quantum-trajectory (monitored) dynamics.\n\nGaussianFermions.jl provides composable primitives for the efficient classical simulation of free-fermion systems. It covers two complementary representations:\n\nNumber-conserving (U(1)) states — SlaterState and CorrelationState, built from the single-particle correlation matrix C_ij = langle c_i^dagger c_jrangle.\nGeneral quadratic (BdG / Majorana) states — MajoranaState, built from the real antisymmetric Majorana covariance matrix, supporting pairing (number-non-conserving) dynamics.\n\nOn top of these states the package implements:\n\nUnitary dynamics via QuadraticHamiltonian / BdGHamiltonian and propagator.\nDeterministic Lindblad dynamics via CorrelationLindblad and MajoranaLindblad, including particle loss/gain, dephasing, and pairing baths.\nQuantum trajectories built from low-level primitives — projective measurement, quantum-jump (MCWF), and continuous (QSD) weak measurement — so you own the trajectory loop and can tune it per script.\nObservables — densities, particle-number statistics, entanglement entropies and spectra, mutual / tripartite information.","title":"GaussianFermions.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"pkg> add https://github.com/jayren3996/GaussianFermions.jl","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-example","page":"Home","text":"using GaussianFermions\n\nL = 16\nH = hopping(L; pbc=true)\n\nloss1 = zeros(ComplexF64, L); loss1[1] = sqrt(0.2)\nlind = CorrelationLindblad(H; loss_ops=[loss1])\n\nstate = CorrelationState(SlaterState(L=L, N=8, config=\"Z2\"))\nevolve!(state, lind, 1.0)\ndensity(state)","title":"Quick example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"New here? Start with Getting Started.\nThe Manual walks through each layer: States, Hamiltonians & Time Evolution, Observables, Correlation-Matrix Lindblad, Majorana / BdG Foundation, and Quantum Trajectories.\nThe API Reference documents every exported function and type.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"#Conventions","page":"Home","text":"Single-particle correlations follow the standard convention C_ij = langle c_i^dagger c_jrangle, F_ij = langle c_i c_jrangle, with the Majorana covariance matrix Gamma_ab = tfraci2langleomega_a omega_brangle. All conventions are verified against exact diagonalization.","title":"Conventions"}]
}
